{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The set spans the three generalization regimes (within-dataset, across datasets, across studies), an order of magnitude in cell-type count (8\u8594 ?86), and both gene-ID conventions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Metrics & protocol\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Per (dataset, method, repeat): {\b accuracy}, {\b macro-F1} (unweighted over classes, so rare types count), {\b ontology-aware concordance} (a call is correct if it is the same node, an ancestor, or a descendant of the truth in the Cell Ontology \u8212- credits right-lineage-wrong-granularity calls; only where both ref and query carry CL ids), {\b fit time}, {\b predict time}, {\b peak RSS}. Each method runs in its own venv via subprocess isolation ({\f1 benchmark/runner.py}); the driver ({\f1 benchmark/driver.py}) builds identical ref/query splits once per dataset and scores every method against them. {\b repeats = 3} (scPRINT: 1, near-deterministic and embed-dominated).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Hardware}: Apple Silicon (M-series), CPU for the classical/linear/correlation tiers and actinn-jax; Apple MPS for the deep and foundation tiers. No discrete GPU, no cloud \u8212- the regime the benchmark is about. Math-library threads are left uncapped (wall-clock as a practitioner sees it); an optional thread cap is available for stricter reproducibility.\par}
