\intbl\row}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 (*scANVI does most of its work in a single train+predict pass, attributed to predict.) Five methods \u8212- SVM, kNN, the linear pipeline, actinn-jax, CellTypist \u8212- predict in well under a second; scTOP and ProtoCloud are ~1\u8211-1.4 s; the deep/correlation tiers are 25\u8211-200\u215 ? slower. actinn-jax predicts in {\b 0.37 s vs.\u160 ?scANVI\u8217's 73 s at statistically tied accuracy} (~195\u215 ?), and is far lighter than the memory-heavy tiers (scmap 8.5 GB, SingleR 3.6 GB).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 The accuracy \u215 ? speed frontier\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw895\pich616\picwgoal9205\pichgoal6336 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Accuracy versus total wall time (liver_intra); actinn-jax sits on the fast frontier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Plotting accuracy against total wall time makes the frontier explicit. The {\b tuned linear pipeline} holds the fast-frontier point: highest matrix accuracy (0.839) at the lowest fit time of any accurate method (4.0 s), placing it above and to the left of actinn-jax, SVM, kNN and CellTypist. The deep methods buy little or nothing on accuracy here \u8212- at or below the linear pipeline on every dataset except lung \u8212- for 50\u8211-200\u215 ? the inference cost. actinn-jax sits just inside the frontier at this reference size; what separates it is not visible on a fixed-size plot but on the scaling axes of \u167 ?3.4 and \u167 ?5: predict time that stays flat as the reference grows, and ~2\u215 ? lower peak memory that holds to atlas scale. The figure is drawn from the liver_intra panel and depicts the ranking in the tables above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Scaling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw1665\pich616\picwgoal17125\pichgoal6336 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Fit and predict time versus reference size and cardinality; predict time stays flat and sub-second.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Training time grows with reference size and with #cell types for all trained methods \u8212- actinn-jax\u8217's fit goes 13 s \u8594 ? 141 s \u8594 ? 254 s as the reference grows 965 \u8594 ? 14.8k \u8594 ? 24k cells, comparable to CellTypist (4 s \u8594 ? 75 s \u8594 ? 246 s) and somewhat above SVM (9 s \u8594 ? 55 s \u8594 ? 80 s); kNN is lazy (fit is trivial but it pays at predict and is the least accurate).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The other axis is the one that matters for the workflows: {\b predict time stays flat and sub-second across the entire range \u8212- ~0.3\u8211-1.4 s whether the reference has 1k or 24k cells, or 5 or 86 types.} Inference cost does not grow with reference size or cardinality, and with the train-once/map-many cache the fit cost is paid once and the flat predict cost is all that recurs \u8212- the regime that matters when a reference is reused across many queries. (The scaling script\u8217's memory column is process-cumulative and not a clean per-size measurement; per-method peak memory is reported from the isolated-subprocess matrix in \u167 ?3.2.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 The large\u8594 ?refined annotation workflow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What actinn-jax is built around is not a single classifier but a {\b two-tier workflow} that matches how annotation is actually done: get a broad call fast, then sharpen it where it matters. Because every stage is a cached {\f1 ReferenceModel} with sub-second, memory-bounded inference (\u167 ?3.2, \u167 ?3.4), the whole workflow runs on a laptop.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Tier 1 \u8212- broad, census-scale.} A shipped ~800-type human reference built from the CELLxGENE census gives any query a first-pass annotation across the whole body, with a {\b calibrated abstain threshold} so out-of-reference cells are flagged rather than force-labeled (see the smooth abstain curve of \u167 ?3.6). This is the \u8220"what am I looking at\u8221" pass \u8212- broad coverage, deliberately not fine-grained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Tier 1 can be built from a model instead of from labels.} The census route above needs a foundation model on a GPU to discover its hierarchy. A pretrained pan-human annotator already has one \u8212- Pan-human Azimuth publishes an 8-level typology with every node mapped to a Cell Ontology term \u8212- so labelling a corpus with it and training actinn-jax on those labels transfers both the vocabulary and the structure. That build needs {\b no accelerator and no labeled input}, only raw human counts: under ten minutes of CPU on 85k cells drawn from three atlases plus a census-wide sample. On 3,396 withheld cross-study liver cells:\par}
