{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 actinn-jax\u8217's probability gives a {\b smooth, tunable} abstain curve \u8212- accuracy on kept cells rises 0.885\u8594 ?0.969 as coverage falls 1.00\u8594 ?0.66 and OOD-flagging climbs 0.00\u8594 ?0.73 \u8212- so a user can dial the precision/coverage/novel-cell-detection trade-off. CellTypist\u8217's probabilities are {\b saturated} (near 0 or 1): the threshold jumps to 68% OOD-flagged at 0.5 and then does not move across 0.5\u8211-0.9, giving essentially one operating point rather than a tunable curve. scmap-cluster offers a single native \u8220"unassigned\u8221" decision (no sweep). A tunable threshold is what makes the abstain step usable as a routing decision in the workflow of \u167 ?3.5.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Foundation-model zero-shot (scPRINT)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 scPRINT is run separately as a zero-shot predictor (no training on the reference), as a reference point for the \u8220"just use a foundation model\u8221" alternative. On the lung datasets it scored {\b exact accuracy 0.027 / ontology concordance 0.206 in 321 s per query} \u8212- vs. actinn-jax\u8217's 0.89 in 0.2 s \u8212- so as a {\i label} predictor it is both slow (~1000\u215 ? actinn-jax) and weak. Its pretrained classifier head also carries a {\b fixed label vocabulary} and requires CL ids, so it cannot emit labels outside that set; this is an inherent property of a zero-shot label head, not a defect, but it means the foundation model\u8217's raw predictions are not a drop-in annotator. This is exactly why our two-stage workflow uses scPRINT\u8217's {\b embeddings} \u8212- its learned structure \u8212- to shape a small trained model, never its zero-shot labels: the foundation model is valuable, its raw label predictions are not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 External validation: Open Problems {\f1 label_projection}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Our in-house benchmark is neutral but self-run. For an {\b independent} check, we ran actinn-jax through the community-standard {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://openproblems.bio/benchmarks/label_projection"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
Open Problems
}}}
 label-projection task \u8212- their 6 datasets, splits, and metrics \u8212- and slotted it into their published v2.0.0 leaderboard.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Placement.} On a benchmark we did not design and cannot tune, {\b actinn-jax places 3rd of 17 on mean accuracy (0.837), 1st among all methods that complete every dataset}, beats its PCA-space {\f1 mlp} sibling, and posts the best accuracy of any completing method on the hardest dataset (tabula_sapiens, 160 types: 0.394 vs mlp 0.342). It is upper-mid on macro-F1 and {\b not the top method overall} \u8212- scANVI+scArches surgery and xgboost score higher on the datasets they finish, though both {\b fail to complete tabula_sapiens}, which is why they rank above actinn-jax only on a mean over the 5 easier datasets. The foundation models again land at the bottom (scgpt_zeroshot 0.639, uce 0.131 \u8776 ? the random-labels control), reproducing \u167 ?3.7 externally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Controlled same-hardware speed & memory.} Leaderboard runtimes mix hardware, so we re-ran actinn-jax and the CPU tier through OP\u8217's {\i own} Nextflow pipeline on a {\b single AWS {\f1 r7i.8xlarge}} \u8212- identical hardware, harness, storage, and trace instrumentation for every method. This is the paper\u8217's cleanest cross-method cost comparison:\par}
