{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Label-free signals for setting the gene budget without test labels. Held-out reference cross-validation and query-cells-per-class both single out tabula_sapiens (where more genes hurt).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Method choice is now dominated by cost, not accuracy.} The five leading methods are separated by 0.008 in mean accuracy and by ~200\u215 ? in inference time (\u167 ?3.1, \u167 ?3.2), so for most annotation jobs the decision is made on the cost axes. On laptop-sized references the tuned linear pipeline is the best of them on accuracy-per-second (0.839, fitting in 4.0 s); at atlas scale ProtoCloud is the most accurate by a clear margin (0.976 vs 0.936 at 49k lung cells; 0.905 vs 0.824 at 47k liver cells), having been below the cluster on the subsampled matrix. actinn-jax\u8217's place in that picture rests on a cost profile with two properties that matter for repeated use rather than for a single labelling run.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Footprint.} Inference is {\b sub-second and flat} \u8212- independent of reference size and cardinality (\u167 ?3.4) \u8212- so a query costs the same against a 1k-cell reference and a 49k-cell one. Peak memory is unremarkable on small references (2.1 GB mean \u8212- seven of the ten other methods are lighter), but it is the lowest of those we carried to {\b atlas scale}: ~2\u215 ? below the linear pipeline (6.1 vs 13.2 GB at 49k lung cells; 6.5 vs 12.6 GB at 47k liver cells), and below scTOP above ~25k cells, where scTOP\u8217's rank processing densifies and crosses over (9.3 vs 6.5 GB at 47k). The advantage is bounded at ~2\u8211-3\u215 ? rather than widening. Combined with the train-once/map-many cache, a reused reference pays fit once and then only the flat predict cost, where the linear pipeline refits scaler/PCA/classifier for every query.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Those two properties are what the workflows are built on.} The practical gains come not from the flat classifier but from the {\b large\u8594 ?refined} pipeline (\u167 ?3.5): a census-scale broad model with a calibrated abstain routes a query to a small focused reference that re-annotates at full resolution (cross-study liver 0.23/0.58 \u8594 ? 0.72/0.86), with zonation as a further tier. The pipeline is only usable because each hop is cheap \u8212- a flat sub-second call against a cached model, at bounded memory \u8212- so several models can be chained, and a large reference kept resident, on a laptop without a GPU. A method that is a few points more accurate but pays a full fit per query, or a multi-gigabyte dense expansion per stage, does not compose the same way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Where it loses, and why.} actinn-jax trails on small references and very-few-cells-per-type sets (\u167 ?3.1, \u167 ?5), and \u8212- a finding from the OP analysis \u8212- it is sensitive to input budget under domain shift: more genes help most datasets but overfit a tiny, fine-grained, shifted query (tabula_sapiens). The two cheapest levers are principled and label-free: {\b standardization} (a scArches-style domain alignment) and a {\b reference-CV-guided gene budget} both improve the model without peeking at test labels, and at a fair gene budget the gene-space MLP {\i matches the leaderboard\u8217's top method} on the datasets both complete \u8212- i.e.\u160 ?much of the apparent gap to heavier methods is representation budget, not model class.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Foundation models.} Their {\b zero-shot labels} are the weakest option in both benchmarks (scPRINT, scGPT, UCE \u8776 ? random); their {\b embeddings/structure} are where the value lies, and our two-stage hierarchy uses exactly that. A cheap CPU shortcut to a foundation-model representation (protein-embedding pooling) did {\i not} transfer (\u167 ?3.8), underscoring that the value is in the trained transformer, not a portable trick. The concurrent {\b Pan-human Azimuth} effort reaches the same conclusion from a far larger curated corpus: a 7M-parameter supervised hierarchical MLP over a 5,055-gene panel yields cleaner annotations than scGPT and SCimilarity, whose labels they find fragmented and mis-transferred. Their stated theme \u8212- that training-data quality and organization matter as much as architecture or scale \u8212- and their finding that accuracy {\b saturates past ~5M training cells} ({\f1 PAN_HUMAN_AZIMUTH.md}) are independent support, from a group with no stake in our conclusion, for both halves of our reading.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i When extra expressivity pays.} Two lines of evidence bear on this. First, {\b ProtoCloud} \u8212- a prototype-based, self-explaining VAE with built-in uncertainty and gene-level attribution, a strictly richer model than ours \u8212- splits by regime rather than by a uniform margin: below the top cluster on the subsampled matrix, ahead on lung, and the strongest method at atlas scale (\u167 ?3.1, \u167 ?5), at ~9\u215 ? actinn-jax\u8217's CPU fit time (146 vs 16 s mean) ({\f1 PROTOCLOUD.md}). The richer model pays off where it has the data to support it, and not before \u8212- which is an argument for matching model capacity to reference size, not for preferring small models everywhere. Second, and more broadly, Souza & Mehta report that {\b parameter-free linear representations} match or exceed single-cell foundation models across cross-species transfer, human cell-type classification and disease-state prediction \u8212- on Tabula Sapiens 2.0 their normalize\u8594 ?ANOVA\u8594 ?PCA\u8594 ?logistic-regression pipeline reaches mean macro-F1 0.899 against 0.907\u8211-0.910 for TranscriptFormer variants \u8212- and they offer a geometric explanation: the biologically realized cell manifold is well approximated by a {\b linear subspace}, so once noise is suppressed performance {\i saturates} and extra expressivity buys little. That is a principled account of why a four-layer MLP over normalized gene space stays competitive, and it matches our own finding that the residual gap to heavier methods was mostly {\b representation budget, not model class} (\u167 ?3.8). Their further result that simple methods do {\i best} out-of-distribution \u8212- novel cell types and unseen organisms \u8212- also rhymes with the rejection behavior we depend on (\u167 ?3.6).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Practical guidance.} For a {\b one-off annotation} on laptop-sized data, use the {\b tuned linear pipeline} (normalize \u8594 ? ANOVA \u8594 ? PCA \u8594 ? logistic regression): it is the most accurate-per-second method here. With a {\b real atlas and time to train}, {\b ProtoCloud} is the most accurate method we benchmarked (0.976 at 49k cells), and the GPU it targets makes its 19\u215 ? CPU fit cost much less punishing in practice. {\b scTOP} suits small, low-cardinality problems, where it is by far the cheapest (~1 s) and competitive. {\b actinn-jax} fits the cases its cost profile is shaped for: when {\b memory is the binding constraint} (~2\u215 ? lighter than the linear pipeline at scale, with memory-bounded chunked inference), when a reference is {\b reused} across many queries (the cached model amortizes the fit), and when the job is the {\b multi-stage workflow} rather than a single label \u8212- broad\u8594 ?refined hand-off, tissue-aware refinement, calibrated abstain, novel-cell-type screening.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For the {\b broad tier specifically, prefer a purpose-built pan-human model}: {\b Pan-human Azimuth} ({\f1 PAN_HUMAN_AZIMUTH.md}) ships a pretrained hierarchical classifier over a harmonized organism-wide typology (8 levels, 382 leaf types, trained on 9.7M curated cells), with abstention learned rather than thresholded and calibration measured at ECE 0.0044, running at ~1,000 cells/s on a laptop. It is better resourced than our census-built reference and we make no claim to improve on its annotations. What we do with it instead is {\b use it}: distilling its labels and its hierarchy into actinn-jax produces a tier-1 model of comparable accuracy at roughly nine times its throughput, built without a GPU and without labeled data (\u167 ?3.5). A curated pan-human model is the right thing to start from; a small fast model is the right thing to iterate with.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The part of any fixed typology that cannot be performed is the {\b hand-off}: it cannot re-annotate into a user\u8217's own focused label set (the HLiCA liver reference, cross-study 0.23/0.58 \u8594 ? 0.72/0.86) or resolve states below a leaf (hepatocyte zonation). ProtoCloud likewise provides uncertainty, gene attribution and a refinement path (fine-tuning a pretrained model). What survives as distinct to actinn-jax is therefore not the breadth of the broad reference but {\b the chain it enables} \u8212- start pan-human, re-annotate into label sets no pretrained model carries, screen what none of them claims \u8212- at a cost that keeps every stage on a laptop. The distillation recipe is not specific to this teacher or to humans: it needs a pretrained annotator whose typology can be read off its outputs, so extending the same entry point to other organisms is a matter of finding one (or building the tier-1 reference from a labeled census slice, as \u167 ?3.5\u8217's census route does).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Limitations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Single hardware family for the in-house panel (Apple Silicon); no discrete-GPU numbers there (deep/foundation tiers would be faster on CUDA \u8212- out of scope for the \u8220"runs-on-a-laptop\u8221" question). The \u167 ?3.8 controlled run covers the {\b CPU tier} on one cloud box; the GPU/R methods there are reported from OP\u8217's own cloud-CI trace (indicative). A same-hardware GPU-tier run to fold those into a single controlled table is the natural extension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Wall-clock comparisons across methods are only meaningful at matched concurrency, and we learned this the expensive way.} A follow-up run that added our four components to the OP harness (\u167 ?3.8) used higher task concurrency and produced runtimes that disagree with the controlled run by up to 12\u215 ? for the same method. Measured {\f1 %cpu} across methods spans 49% to 2338% \u8212- some are single-threaded, some saturate 23 cores \u8212- so any wall-clock ranking silently ranks threading and scheduler contention alongside algorithm. The controlled low-concurrency run is the one we report; the extension establishes only that the components run and what memory they need.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Subsampled references (to keep the matrix tractable); the scaling section characterizes the size dependence directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Human only; six datasets per benchmark; GPU foundation models beyond scPRINT/UCE (scGPT, Geneformer, popV) not run locally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab actinn-jax needs more cells/type than linear methods to reach parity (\u167 ?3.1); on very small references it trails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b The distilled tier-1 reference inherits a vocabulary, not a calibration.} Pan-human Azimuth\u8217's abstention is trained (and its ECE measured at 0.0044); the distilled student learns hard labels only, so it carries none of that \u8212- its confidence barely separates right from wrong (\u167 ?3.5). Recalibrating it, or distilling from soft targets, is the obvious next step and would need a loss change in the package. Its evaluation is also two withheld {\i atlases}, not withheld tissue: the census-wide corpus spans 376 tissues, so nothing here measures behaviour on biology the corpus never saw.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b The gene budget is dataset-dependent, not a free parameter.} More genes help most references but {\i overfit} a tiny, fine-grained, domain-shifted query (tabula_sapiens \u8722 ?10 pt); our reference-CV + cells-per-class selection rule is validated on 6 datasets with a single clean failure case, so it is directional evidence, not a tuned threshold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Standardization is shipped opt-in}, not default, because it shifts probability calibration that the two-stage abstain thresholds are tuned against; combining the two cleanly would require re-tuning those thresholds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Our classical tier (SVM, kNN, CellTypist) is untuned, and a tuned linear baseline beats actinn-jax.} Souza & Mehta\u8217's pipeline (normalize \u8594 ? ANOVA \u8594 ? standardize \u8594 ? PCA(220) \u8594 ? logistic regression) leads the matrix on accuracy (0.839 vs 0.831) and macro-F1 (0.699 vs 0.683) while fitting 4\u215 ? faster, with its largest margin on the 86-type blood+gut set (+4.2 pt) ({\f1 SIMPLE_BASELINES.md}). The \u167 ?3.1 margins over the classical tier should therefore be read as margins over {\i untuned} baselines; a like-for-like tuning effort on SVM or CellTypist would likely narrow them further. scTOP is cheaper still on time (~1 s) but competitive only at small, low cardinality \u8212- best pbmc macro-F1 (0.837), degrading to 0.649 on liver.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b actinn-jax\u8217's memory advantage is bounded, and the linear pipeline scales further than extrapolation suggests} ({\f1 SCALING_MEMORY.md}). Extrapolating the linear pipeline\u8217's small-reference footprint predicts ~32 GB and failure at atlas scale; measured, it uses {\b 13.2 GB} and completes, because {\f1 SelectKBest(k=20000)} caps the dense matrix well below the full gene set. The linear/actinn-jax memory ratio is likewise {\b bounded at ~2\u8211-3\u215 ?} (2.15\u215 ? at full scale) rather than widening with data, so there is no scaling cliff and no regime tested where the linear pipeline stops being usable. Memory projections in this setting should be measured, not extrapolated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b At atlas scale the ordering changes and the deep model leads.} Scaling the lung reference 3k \u8594 ? 49k cells, {\b ProtoCloud goes from worst (0.722) to best (0.976)}, clear of actinn-jax (0.936) and the linear pipeline (0.939), at 19\u215 ? the CPU fit cost; scTOP gains nothing from scale (0.819 \u8594 ? 0.846) and loses its memory edge (1.22\u215 ? actinn-jax at 49k). This replicates on the HLiCA liver atlas (36 types, 525k cells, sweep to 47k ref): same bounded ~2\u215 ? memory band, same ProtoCloud reversal (0.581 \u8594 ? 0.907), same scTOP stagnation (0.657 \u8594 ? 0.583), with scTOP crossing over to heavier than actinn-jax (1.41\u215 ?). Conclusions drawn from subsampled references do not transfer to atlas scale, in either direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Annotation only.} Cross-species transfer and disease-state prediction \u8212- the tasks where Souza & Mehta find the largest foundation-model deficits, and where a fast method would be most attractive \u8212- are outside this benchmark\u8217's scope (human, within/cross-dataset annotation).\sa180\par}
